fact WorkItem(id: "WI-850", description: some(value: "`operation foo[T = Int64](...)`: the type-param DEFAULT is parsed, recorded in the IR, and then SILENTLY DROPPED by the loader — decide whether it is a feature and honour it, or refuse it at load.\n\nWHAT I MEASURED (HEAD b0411496, 2026-07-28). The parse IR carries it: `parse/ir.rs:544-550`, `pub struct TypeParam { name: Symbol, default: Option<TypeExpr>, span: Span }`, whose doc reads \"Default value from `[T = Int]`; `None` for bare `[T]` (which the loader treats as `T = ?` — a fresh anonymous logical variable)\". The parser FILLS it — `anthill-core/tests/include/parse_test.rs:4797-4808` asserts `op.type_params[0].default` is `Some(...)` for a defaulted param, and :4783 asserts `None` for a bare one. The loader NEVER READS it: `load_operation` (kb/load.rs:13387-13401) mints a fresh var per param from the NAME alone (`kb.fresh_var(tp_sym)`), ignoring `tp.default`; `scan_operation_params` (kb/load.rs:1704-1709) reads only `tp.name`. A grep for `.default` across the whole of kb/load.rs finds no `TypeParam` read at all. So the field is write-only.\n\nCONSEQUENCE. `operation foo[T = Int64](x: T) -> T` loads EXACTLY as if written `operation foo[T](x: T) -> T`. The default is accepted by the grammar, stored in the IR, and has no effect anywhere in the KB, the typer, or codegen. The user-visible failure is worse than inert: a call that leaves `T` otherwise unconstrained raises `UnconstrainedTypeParam` (kb/typing.rs:13163-13195), whose message tells the author to pin it with `op[T = ...]` — when they already wrote that pin ON THE DECLARATION. Silent acceptance of a construct with no semantics is the exact shape CLAUDE.md's \"prefer a loud error over a silent skip\" rule exists for, and passing parse tests are what hid it (parse_test covers that the default PARSES; nothing covers that it DOES anything).\n\nDECIDE FIRST, DO NOT GUESS. Read docs/kernel-language.md and docs/proposals/ BEFORE coding — the sort side has both `sort T = ?` and `sort T = Concrete`, so there may already be an intended reading for the operation side that the loader simply never implemented. Two admissible outcomes:\n  (a) HONOUR IT. The default becomes the seed for `T` when nothing else constrains it — neither a call-site `[T = ...]` (`seed_op_type_args` / `resolve_call_type_arg_targets`), nor the expected type, nor arg unification — i.e. it is consulted at exactly the point `check_unconstrained_type_params` currently raises, turning that error into a fill. This requires carrying the default INTO the type-param table as an ADDED field beside the var (an `Option<Value>`, NOT a re-typing of the var itself), threading it through the `OperationInfo` fact, and a decision on whether a default may mention an earlier param (`[T, U = List[T]]`) — say yes or no explicitly. Coordinate with the sibling ticket that re-types that table to `Vec<(Symbol, Var)>`: this is the one place the table legitimately needs to carry a TYPE as well as a var, and the two changes touch the same three struct definitions.\n  (b) REFUSE IT AT LOAD, with a located error naming the operation and the parameter, until (a) is designed. Cheap, honest, and reversible.\nEither is acceptable. Silently dropping is not.\n\nWORK. Decide (a) or (b) with the spec in hand; implement it; test the defaulted declaration END TO END — a driven load + call, not another parse assertion. Whichever way it goes, replace the doc comment on `TypeParam.default` with the verdict and its reason, since the current text (\"Default value from `[T = Int]`\") is the artifact that reads as though the loader honours it.\n\nACCEPTANCE: `operation foo[T = Int64](x: T) -> T` either (a) type-checks with `T` filling to `Int64` at an otherwise-unconstrained call — pinned by a test that FAILS on today's HEAD — or (b) fails to load with an error naming the op and the param; `TypeParam.default`'s doc states the verdict; full workspace green via rustland/scripts/test.sh.\n\nSOURCE: found 2026-07-28 while auditing `OperationInfoFull.type_params` — while establishing that every entry in that table is a plain variable, `TypeParam.default` turned out to be the ONE place the table would legitimately need to carry a type instead.\n\nSCOPE: rustland/anthill-core/src/{parse/ir.rs, kb/load.rs, kb/typing.rs}, docs/kernel-language.md."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-28T20:20:26Z"))
